"full_text": "Yemen continues to face one of the most complex humanitarian and political crises globally, with recurrent conflict, disrupted health systems and constrained operational access. Maintenance of even basic public health functions in this environment is challenging.\nOver the past several years, support from the Pandemic Influenza Preparedness (PIP) Partnership Contribution (PC) has played an instrumental role in sustaining, modernizing, and progressively strengthening Yemen’s health security and influenza preparedness capacities. Rather than financing short-term interventions, PIP PC funds have successfully driven a strategic transition toward a structured, standardized, and sustainable approach to national preparedness.\nThe 2024–2025 biennium marked a key milestone in this transition, focusing on consolidating systems, strengthening coordination and embedding preparedness into national structures.\nStrengthening surveillance and reconnecting globally\nA major achievement has been the establishment of standardized national operational guidelines and procedures for sentinel influenza surveillance. PIP funding spearheaded the development of the National Surveillance Protocol and comprehensive Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for influenza and other respiratory tract infections, while simultaneously supporting the establishment of National Rapid Response Teams (RRTs). The strength of this institutional framework was proven during the COVID-19 pandemic, when Yemen’s National Pandemic Influenza Preparedness Plan (2019–2023) provided the essential blueprint for the country's crisis response. During the acute phase of the pandemic, existing influenza sentinel networks were successfully leveraged as the core structural and data-reporting framework, ultimately culminating in the integration of COVID-19 and influenza into a single, cohesive national respiratory response plan.\nTo bridge nation
