"full_text": "Building the foundations for pandemic preparedness\nImplementation of the Pandemic Influenza Preparedness (PIP) Framework Partnership Contribution (PC) in Armenia began in 2014 and has since driven measurable improvements in influenza surveillance and response capacities, contributing to the country's long-term pandemic preparedness.\nThe support provided by PIP PC to strengthen sentinel surveillance in Armenia marked a turning point, establishing a systematic approach to influenza surveillance. Standardized case definitions and reporting procedures were introduced, aligning national practices with global standards and creating a robust, data-driven mechanism for monitoring trends in respiratory infections, conducting risk assessments and informing decision-making.\nPIP support expanded Armenia’s sentinel surveillance network, increasing the number of sites from 8 to 14. The introduction of standard procedures, combined with regular training and continuous feedback mechanisms, resulted in improved case detection, sampling and reporting practices. Over time, influenza surveillance was integrated with surveillance for other respiratory pathogens, culminating in the establishment of a comprehensive, integrated national respiratory surveillance system.\nThe improvements in data quality driven by PIP PC have strengthened Armenia’s capacity for seasonal influenza risk assessment, enhanced monitoring of virus circulation, and enabled timelier, evidence-based public health action. Today, sentinel surveillance data from Armenia inform regional and global platforms, ensuring that Armenia’s situation contributes to the broader global health intelligence network.\nEnhancing laboratory capacity and integration\nWith PIP PC funding, influenza laboratory capacity in Armenia has been significantly strengthened and the National Center for Disease Control and Prevention (NCDC) Reference Laboratory obtain
