"date_iso": "2026-04-17T00:00:00",
"full_text": "Laying the groundwork for regional preparedness plans\nWhen countries across the WHO South‑East Asia Region (SEAR) began national influenza pandemic planning as early as 2006, global guidance, tools and standardized frameworks were still in their infancy. Yet Member States moved forward with determination, often relying on country‑driven, locally developed approaches that varied in scope and structure but shared a common objective: to be ready for the next influenza pandemic.\nThe adoption of the Pandemic Influenza Preparedness (PIP) Framework in 2011 marked a decisive turning point. For the first time, countries had access to predictable financing and a coherent global architecture for preparedness, anchored in successive High-Level Implementation Plans (HLIP I–III). This transformed pandemic planning from a fragmented national effort into a structured, results‑driven exercise.\nBacked by sustained PIP PC funding, all Member States in the Region developed or updated their National Influenza Pandemic Preparedness Plans (NIPPPs) in line with WHO’s Pandemic Influenza Risk Management guidance. By the end of HLIP II (2018–2023), every country had achieved this milestone, reflecting the Region’s long-term commitment and marking a major shared accomplishment in strengthening influenza pandemic preparedness.\nThis achievement stands as the Region’s testament to long-term commitment and represents a key shared accomplishment in strengthening influenza pandemic preparedness.\nWith the introduction of the WHO Preparedness and Resilience for Emerging Threats (PRET) initiative, and supported by Output 1 (Policy and Plans) of HLIP‑III, Member States have begun transitioning from influenza‑specific preparedness to broader respiratory pandemic planning. The Region has set an ambitious target: all Member States will have fully updated PRET‑aligned influenza and other respiratory pathogen pandemic prepa
